nodecode-nachos is a rewrite of Nacho-Labs-LLC's Nacho-Labs-LLC/hermes-plugin-nachos:

  https://github.com/Nacho-Labs-LLC/hermes-plugin-nachos
  commit 2ad0b272d4fbd2c000713e927a656851dc8787e2
  MIT License, Copyright (c) 2026 Nacho Labs LLC (LICENSE)

The plugin was a Hermes memory provider and context engine: one titled-markdown
or SQLite store of memory entries, a manifest injected into every turn, a
lexical prefetch of the entries a turn's query ranks highest, and three model
tools to put, recall and remove an entry. This rewrite keeps that shape in
Common Lisp with no dependency beyond Nodecode: the flat-file markdown format
is the original's (# category, ## title, first body line as the summary, keys
as the slug of the title, later duplicates winning, a heading inside a title
or body refused as structure), the manifest renders every entry and only ever
shortens summaries under its budget, the prefetch is the same smoothed TF-IDF
over titles and summaries with the same character budget and the same mark in
the manifest, and the three tools keep their names, arguments and JSON
answers. The manifest rides every request as the cell's own harness section;
the prefetch runs at each round against the turn's own words and hands the
ranked bodies to that round's request. What was left out: the SQLite store
(the one markdown file is hand-editable and is what the original defaulted
flat-file users to), the opt-in semantic scorer and its three embedding
backends -- the lexical scorer is the default and asks nothing of the network
-- the pre-compaction snapshot checkpoints, the context-engine compaction
itself, and the experimental YAML policy plugin, all Hermes-runtime seams
Nodecode's own context budget and compaction already cover. Nothing of the
original is vendored: there was no code to vendor, only behaviour to rewrite.
